2026-10-19 source check: Chabassier, Chaigne and Joly (M2AN 48, 2014, section 4) apply the law to EACH string of a choir:
- (4.4) e_i(t) = (xi_bar - xi_i(t) + xi(t))^+ per string;
- (4.5) F_i = k_H Phi_H(e_i);
- (4.6) adds r_H d/dt Phi_H(e_i);
- (4.7) Phi_H(y) = y^p, p 'between 1.5 and 3.5 to fit the experimental behavior' (their ref [29]).
So per-string K_H is the source model, not our misreading, and the string-dominated treble contact follows from RT-0425's published K_H(i) = 10^(0.0531 i + 7.64). That is 4.0e9 N/m^p at A3 (p 2.39) and 1.1e11 at C6 (p 2.84), i.e. about 280 N per string at 1 mm compression at A3.

The open physical question is whether that treble K_H matches measured treble hammers. Next: compare it with Stulov's measured hammer parameters (Proc. Estonian Acad. Sci. Eng. 1995/2000, kirj.ee), and with Russell's and Giordano's force-compression data, before touching any law. The paper text is at scratchpad papers/m2an-2014-chabassier.pdf / .txt.

2026-10-19 measured hammer data: Stulov & Magi, 'Piano hammer testing device', Proc. Estonian Acad. Sci. Eng. 6(4) 2000, 259-267 (doi 10.3176/eng.2000.4.01; page images at scratchpad papers/stulov-2000-p*.png).

Renner A1 hammer on a 4.3 mm string, 0.35-0.78 m/s, hysteretic model:
- F0 = 400 N/mm^p (6.3e9 N/m^p), p = 2.4, eps 0.51, tau0 400 us;
- about 75 N at 0.46 mm.

Abel A1 hammer at 0.7 m/s:
- on a flat surface: F0 480 N/mm^p, p 2.7;
- on strings of d = 4.3 / 2.25 / 1.25 mm: F0 = 400 / 330 / 270 N/mm^p, p = 2.3 / 2.0 / 1.8.

So the same felt is softer and less nonlinear on thinner strings, and treble strings are about 1 mm. RT-0425 does the opposite:
- its p rises to 2.84 at C6;
- its K_H rises exponentially, to 1.1e11 N/m^p at C6 (about 330 N per string at 1 mm);
- at A1 it gives 2.15e8 N/m^2.28, about 31 N per string at 1 mm, roughly 13x softer than Stulov's measured A1 hammer.

A law justified by data would need treble hammer measurements on treble-diameter strings, plus the contact-geometry dependence Stulov shows (a line contact of a d-mm wire indenting felt), rather than a per-key exponential fit. Recorded for whoever picks this up. Not changing the law without treble data.

description:
The live kernel's strings are linear. The offline reference has geometric extension with finite longitudinal waves (grand_piano/longitudinal_string.rs + string_stretching.rs, --string-stretching waves). The graded renders use it on A3 only (axial-a3.fspx).

Measured 2026-10-19 (offline A3 on the POD-128 board with air, 2-4 kHz share of 50 Hz-16 kHz, first second after onset):
- waves: -40.4 / -33.0 / -28.0 / -22.1 dB at 0.6 / 2 / 4.5 / 8 m/s;
- linear: -33.3 / -30.6 / -29.3 at 2 / 4.5 / 8 m/s;
- real Iowa A3: pp -46.9, mf -32.3, ff -20.3.
So waves add +2.6 dB at 4.5 m/s and +7.2 dB at 8 m/s, a share of the ff brightness that grows with amplitude (A3's first longitudinal mode is ~3.3 kHz). The live engine at 4 m/s reads -30.4 dB. At C6 the first longitudinal mode is ~14 kHz, and waves move it by 0.7 dB (8xcsp), so this is a mid/bass lever, not the C6 fix.

Port the reference's waves to the live kernel's sounding strings, for keys with an authored axial rigidity:
- plain steel (MIDI 41-108 in this scale): EA = E*A with A from the scale's EI, as axial-a3.fspx does;
- wound strings need authored EA and are out of scope until that data exists.
Keep parity with the reference (validate.rs with the same spec) to the existing 4e-12 tolerance per key.
Measure the per-sample cost in V8 against the board-size lever: a waves string should not cost more than the board coordinates it would displace.

Done when:
- validate.rs parity holds with waves on for A3, C4 and C5;
- the live A3 at 4.5 and 8 m/s matches the offline 2-4 kHz share within 0.5 dB;
- the cost is stated on the page, and the page lists what is and is not covered (wound strings).

