description:
66w1c measured that the live piano's silent-string gap needs the damped silent strings' low partials as dynamic modes. Static images fail, and a broadband dashpot fails too: the load is resonant and partial-specific.

What it buys: every silent partial <= 300 Hz (277 modes, 102 segments) brings A3 and C4 within 0.5 and 0.8 dB of the reference at 2-8 kHz, and their early decays within 1 dB/s; without them, C4 is 5.1 dB dull. Partials <= 600 Hz (665 modes) reach -24 to -27 dB waveform error.

Through the generic course path it costs 2.6x (r128) to 5.7x (r32) per sample, because every course pays about 12 r-length coupling operations.

Build the bank as its own LTI subsystem in engine-piano kernel.rs:
- always attached, damped by its pads, which lift with the sustain pedal;
- one gather (b = G^T x) and one scatter per step over the silent bridge rows;
- modal intrinsic loss without the bridge-coupled strain terms;
- folded into the bridge Schur as a precomputed dense inverse, with Woodbury for sounding keys on top;
- outside max_courses and max_dynamic_modes, never retired;
- a struck silent key extends to its full grant, energy-continuously, and returns to the bank when retired.
The energy books must close, with the dampers' share in damper_loss_j.

Done when:
- the native live A3/C4/C6 sits within 1 dB at 2-8 kHz and 1 dB/s in early decay of the all-silent-strings reference (scratchpad fidelity/passive method);
- validate.rs parity holds with the bank off;
- the wasm build's measured per-sample cost at r32 and r64 is stated, and the browser's board/budget choice accounts for it;
- the page's silent-strings bullet is re-measured. 
Context: frankensim-66w1c comments (2026-10-19).

